[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I investigated for a control-plane “summary into itself” loop but found the routing domain behaving correctly end to end. Evidence:
- Data-plane: pc0 ↔ pc12 ping succeeds both ways. Traceroutes from both ends to multiple “far” loopbacks (e.g., 14.1.1.1, 50.1.1.1, 70.1.1.1, 80.1.1.1, 90.1.1.1) show clean, monotonic paths via the expected ABRs, with no TTL bouncing or path oscillation.
- OSPF: Inter-area routes look sane. Example: r1 reaches 1.0.0.0/8 via r3 (31.1.1.1); r3 reaches 1.0.0.0/8 via r2 (23.1.1.1); r4 reaches 1.0.0.0/8 via r3 (34.1.1.1). No self-referential next-hops observed. Area 4 stub default is present only inside the stub (r10 has 0/0 via r2), not leaked elsewhere.
- EIGRP/OSPF boundary: r11 correctly learns 102.x/113.x and 120/8 via EIGRP (nexthops 112.1.1.2 and 103.1.1.2) and redistributes them into OSPF; there are no routes on r11 pointing back to its own address (validated: 113.1.1.0/24 via 103.1.1.2, 102.1.1.0/24 via 112.1.1.2). r12 and r13 default to r11 as expected.
- End-to-end from EIGRP side: pc12 to OSPF loopbacks and to r10 (100.1.1.1) succeeds with sensible paths.

Mechanism suspected by the report (a summary that resolves to itself) would present as routes with next-hop = local interface or ping/traceroute ping-pong; we did not find such entries or behavior. Root cause: none active; likely a transient convergence observation. Corrective fix: none required. As a precaution, avoid mutual redistribution without filtering and monitor OSPF LSAs if intermittent issues recur. 
=== END ===